course: dictionary
lesson: 12467
title: Deflation: air, money and feelings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Deflation has more than one meaning, so the words around it tell you which one you need. If the sentence is about tyres, balloons or air, it means letting air out so something gets smaller and softer. «The deflation of the bouncy castle took ten minutes.» The air leaves, so the castle sinks slowly.
explain: In a money lesson, deflation means prices falling in a country over time. It is the opposite of inflation, when prices rise. «Deflation meant the shops sold their goods for less money.» Check by swapping in your meaning: does "prices falling" still make sense in the sentence? If it does, you have the right sense.
explain: Deflation can also describe feelings. «After losing the final, Sam felt a sudden sense of deflation.» The words sudden and losing show he was disappointed. Read the whole sentence, and the clues will tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Deflation means letting air or gas out, so something gets smaller and softer.
fact: In economics, deflation means a general fall in prices in a country over time.
fact: Deflation is the opposite of inflation, which is when prices rise.
fact: A sense of deflation is a sudden feeling of disappointment or loss of confidence.
fact: Falling prices can sound good, but they can mean businesses earn less and people lose jobs.
exercise: «The bouncy castle slowly showed deflation as the air leaked out.» What does deflation mean here? | Painting the castle a new colour | *Letting the air out, so it gets smaller and softer | Blowing the air in hard so it grows bigger | Bouncing very high in the air
exercise: In a money lesson, what does deflation mean? | A bank where people keep their savings safe | A new coin made of gold | *A general fall in prices over time | A rise in prices every year
exercise: «After the match, the team had a sense of deflation.» What does this suggest? | Full of energy and ready to run | A need to pump up the football | *Sudden disappointment | Great excitement and joy
test: Which word is the opposite of deflation in economics? | Deflation of a tyre | *Inflation | Disappointment | Confidence
test: «The tyre showed deflation after the nail went in.» Which meaning fits? | A large increase in the cost of food | *Air leaking out, so the tyre gets softer | Prices falling in a shop | A sudden feeling of happiness
exam: Why might falling prices (deflation) worry a country? | It means prices have risen sharply | *It can mean businesses earn less and people lose jobs | It always makes everyone richer and happier | It means the tyres are full of air
